# Yamlover meta - the meta language, yamlover's replacement for JSON Schema. Root body:
# what meta is and what it drives; each part a subdirectory chapter, ordered by the pointer
# array below. Absorbed from language/logical-graph/metadata (+ facets) - log in ../DOCSMIGRATION.md.
Yamlover meta
description: the meta language - the replacement for JSON Schema: typing, description, presentation
- >
  **Yamlover meta** is yamlover's schema language - and its **replacement for JSON Schema**. It keeps
  a familiar-looking vocabulary (`type`, `format`, and the [members clause](*:members) that fuses
  `properties`/`items`/`prefixItems`) but repurposes it: the primary purpose is **metadata** -
  typing, decoding, and presentation - with validation a secondary, optional use
  ([metadata first](*::language:principles:metadata-first)). Data lives in the instance; the meta only
  describes it. Schema-as-storage - pinning data with `const` so the schema *is* the instance - is
  the one old model that was dropped.
- >
  **The three-way split.** A **concrete** is a concrete representation *and* the language or codec
  that translates it into the abstract value ([concretes](*::language:concretes)) - `yamlover/stream`
  parses a file's text, `binary/int32/le` decodes four bytes into an integer, `base64` turns text
  into bytes. A **type** is the kind of the abstract value - and it is usually *inferred by the
  concrete's interpreter*; stating `type:` alone means "read whatever you have so this type comes
  out" (`type: binary` keeps a file opaque). A **format** is a named **constraint** on the abstract
  value - `image/png` on bytes, `text/markdown` on a string - and it **never selects decoding**.
  (Before the split, `format` carried the decoding too - `format: int32/le`, `format: yamlover/meta`;
  that spelling reads forever but is not authored anymore.)
- >
  **What the metadata drives.** *Decoding*: a member's `concrete:` says how to turn its
  representation into a value. *Rendering*: the viewer picks a renderer from the facets and the
  format. *Presentation*: `title` and `description` annotate a node without living in its data.
  Concrete resolution order: the meta `concrete:` if stated - it always wins; else a recognized file
  extension; else - no meta and no extension - a sniff: textual bytes parse as yamlover, binary
  bytes stay an opaque blob (`application/octet-stream`).
- >
  This chapter is the meta reference, shaped like the JSON Schema book: [attaching](*:attaching) a
  meta to an instance; the [three facets](*:facets) - the type system that replaces the `type`
  keyword's boxes; the per-facet keyword chapters - [value](*:value) and the
  [members clause](*:members); [enumerated and constant values](*:enum-const);
  [annotations](*:annotations); [formats](*:format); [composition](*:composition);
  [conditionals](*:conditionals); [structuring](*:structuring) - `$defs`, pointers, hosted schemas;
  the [principles](*:principles) behind the design; and the keyword-by-keyword
  [comparison with JSON Schema](*:vs-json-schema).
